# UniFi backup recovery

This directory contains age-encrypted UniFi backups of two kinds:

| File | Holds |
|---|---|
| `<prefix>-YYYY-MM-DD-system.unifi.age` | **System Config Backup** (`.unifi`): UniFi OS, its local admins, Control Plane settings and every application, Network included |
| `<prefix>-YYYY-MM-DD-network-<version>.unf.age` | **Network backup** (`.unf`): the Network application only (networks, Wi-Fi, firewall, routing, adopted devices, clients) |

`<version>` is the Network application version that made the `.unf`. Which
kinds exist depends on the job's `BACKUP_KINDS`.

## Which file to use

- **Default: restore the newest `.unifi`.** It brings back UniFi OS and the
  Network application together, and it can be restored onto a different
  console model as long as that console runs UniFi OS 3.1 or newer.
- **Only the Network configuration is broken, UniFi OS is fine: restore the
  `.unifi` and select only the Network application.** A System Config Backup
  can be restored partially; the rest of UniFi OS (admins, Control Plane
  settings, other applications) stays as it is.
- **Fallback: restore a `.unf`** when no `.unifi` is available or it won't
  restore, or onto a self-hosted Network application. Afterwards recreate by
  hand what it lacks (local admins, Control Plane settings, other
  applications).

No backup restores onto an **older** application version. A `.unf` needs
the same or a newer Network application than the version in its filename.
A restore can't add hardware features the replacement console lacks.

## To decrypt one file

You need an age identity (private key) whose public key was listed in
`AGE_RECIPIENTS` at backup time.

```sh
age -d -i /path/to/your.key -o backup.unifi <prefix>-YYYY-MM-DD-system.unifi.age
age -d -i /path/to/your.key -o backup.unf <prefix>-YYYY-MM-DD-network-<version>.unf.age
```

Both formats contain Wi-Fi passwords, admin password hashes and VPN keys.
Keep them on an encrypted volume only and shred them when you're done.

### If the recipient was a YubiKey

If the backup was encrypted to a YubiKey recipient (`age1yubikey1...`), you
need both [`age`](https://age-encryption.org/) and
[`age-plugin-yubikey`](https://github.com/str4d/age-plugin-yubikey) installed,
a running PC/SC daemon (`pcscd`), and one of the YubiKeys that was listed as a
recipient inserted.

```sh
# With the YubiKey inserted, export a stub identity for it:
age-plugin-yubikey --identity > yubikey-identity.txt

# Decrypt (you will be prompted for the PIN and a touch):
age -d -i yubikey-identity.txt -o <backup file> <prefix>-YYYY-MM-DD-...age

# The identity stub holds no secret (just a pointer to the hardware slot):
rm yubikey-identity.txt
```

Any one of the YubiKeys listed at backup time works — you do not need all of
them.

## To restore

Swap in the replacement gateway or console and leave the other UniFi devices
connected as they were.

### A `.unifi`

1. Restore it during the console's first setup, or later from Settings >
   Control Plane > Backups > Restore from Backup, and upload `backup.unifi`.
2. Pick the applications and settings to restore (all of them for a full
   recovery, only Network for a Network-only rollback).
3. Wait for every device to come back online.

### A `.unf`

1. The first-setup restore screen only accepts a `.unifi`: skip it and set
   the console up fresh.
2. Update the Network application to at least the version in the filename.
3. Settings > Control Plane > Backups > Restore from Backup, and upload
   `backup.unf`.
4. Wait for every device to come back online. If they're on a different
   Layer 3 subnet from the new console and don't show up, enable Override
   Inform Host in the Network application and enter the console's IP.

Ubiquiti's guide: <https://help.ui.com/hc/en-us/articles/360008976393>
("Backups and Migration in UniFi").
